ng controlling. - Help him explore his interests deeply. Passion is magnetic; when someone talks about something they love, shyness often melts away, and others are drawn to that energy. ### 4. Healthy dating mindset - Instead of trying to be “the kind of man women want,” shift to: “How can I connect with someone who appreciates me as I am?” That filters out incompatible matches early. - Rejection is normal. Teach him not to take it personally. Every “no” is just a step toward a better fit. ### What to avoid - Advice that frames dating as a game where men must “take control” or treat women as a monolith. That approach often reads as pushy or insecure, and can push away the very people who’d enjoy his gentle nature. - “Fake it till you make it” with a macho persona. That’s exhausting and impossible to maintain; eventually the crack shows, and trust breaks. If he has deep social anxiety or low self-esteem, it might also be worth suggesting therapy or a assertiveness training course—not to make him dominant, but to help him feel comfortable expressing his needs and boundaries. You clearly care about him. The best coaching you can give is to remind him he’s enough as he is, and support him in growing the tools to let the right person see that.
